    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p11-definition",
     "replacement": "A **composite** function is formed when the output of one function is used as the input to another function. The composite is defined only for inputs $x$ in the domain of the inner function whose output is in the domain of the outer function.\n\nA function involving two or more successive operations can be viewed as a composite function.\n\nIn $f(g(x))$,\t$g$ is the **inner function** (evaluated first) and $f$ is the **outer function** (evaluated last).\n\nWe can write the composite function as $f\\circ g$, with $(f\\circ g)(x)=f(g(x))$. The notation $fg(x)$ is also used when composition is understood.\n\nWe read $f(g(x))$ as ‘$f$ of $g$’.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p11-definition",
     "targetFingerprint": "eca777e117da5973df8adc259fac300019aaf7a0876f5fb3127496a2fad18b8d"
    }
   ],
